---

<Base
  title="What OpenTelemetry's GenAI Conventions Can and Can't Tell You About Your Agents, KYDE"
  description="The gen_ai.* conventions give every agent framework a shared way to describe what happened in a run. Deciding whether a run was normal is a separate problem, and it needs a baseline, not another attribute."
  datePublished="2026-11-07"
>
  <Navbar />

  <main>

    <!-- ══════════ HEADER ══════════ -->
    <section class="relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="bg-grid absolute inset-0 pointer-events-none"></div>
      <div class="relative max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 pt-36 pb-16 md:pt-48 md:pb-20 md:border-x md:border-line-0">

        <div class="mb-10">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
            <a href="/resources/" class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-ink-1 hover:text-ink-1 transition-colors">↑ Resources</a>
            <span class="text-ink-1">/</span>
            <span class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-ink-1">Technical</span>
          </div>
          <div class="h-px w-full bg-line-0"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="max-w-3xl">
          <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold text-ink-0 tracking-tight leading-[1.03] mb-6">
            What OpenTelemetry's GenAI Conventions Can and Can't Tell You About Your Agents
          </h1>
          <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-xs font-mono text-ink-1">
            <span>Technical</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>6 min read</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <time datetime="2026-11-07">November 2026</time>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ══════════ ARTICLE BODY ══════════ -->
    <section class="border-t border-line-0">
      <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 md:py-24 md:border-x md:border-line-0">
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,42rem)_280px] gap-6 md:gap-16 items-start">

          <!-- Body -->
          <article class="prose-kyde min-w-0">

            <p>
              If you run AI agents in production, the <a href="https://github.com/open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry GenAI semantic conventions</a> deserve your attention. They define a shared vocabulary, under the <code>gen_ai.*</code> namespace, for describing model calls, tool calls, retrieval, memory and complete agent runs. A run becomes a trace, each model call and tool call becomes a span, and token usage becomes a metric.
            </p>
            <p>
              Adoption is uneven, but it is moving in one direction. VS Code Copilot already emits <code>invoke_agent</code>, <code>chat</code> and <code>execute_tool</code> spans that follow the conventions. Claude Code adds a handful of <code>gen_ai.*</code> attributes to its tracing, which is still in beta. Several older instrumentation libraries predate the conventions and are moving toward them gradually.
            </p>
            <p>
              That is useful progress. But a shared way to describe what an agent did does not tell you whether it should have done it. The second question is the one most teams need answered, and the conventions leave it open by design.
            </p>

            <h2>What the conventions standardize</h2>
            <p>
              The central attribute is <code>gen_ai.operation.name</code>, which says what kind of work a span represents: <code>chat</code>, <code>embeddings</code>, <code>invoke_agent</code>, <code>execute_tool</code>, <code>retrieval</code>, or one of the other values the spec defines. A few more attributes do most of the remaining work:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><span><code>gen_ai.provider.name</code> and <code>gen_ai.request.model</code> record which provider and model answered.</span></li>
              <li><span><code>gen_ai.tool.name</code> and <code>gen_ai.tool.call.id</code> record which tool the agent called.</span></li>
              <li><span><code>gen_ai.conversation.id</code> ties the spans of one session together.</span></li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              MCP fits the same model. The spec treats an MCP tool call as an <code>execute_tool</code> span, so a tool behind a protocol and a tool in your own process look the same in a trace.
            </p>
            <p>
              The conventions also make a deliberate choice about content. Instrumentations should not capture prompts and responses by default, and should offer a way to opt in when you need them. As a result, the most useful operational signal, which tools an agent called, in what order, how long each took and whether it failed, is available without anyone storing what was said.
            </p>

            <h2>What a single span can't show</h2>
            <p>
              Each span describes one step on its own. Take the trace most guides use as an example: an <code>invoke_agent</code> run that calls the model, runs <code>web_search</code>, calls the model again, runs <code>summarize_doc</code> and calls the model one last time. Every span is well formed, and every attribute matches the spec.
            </p>
            <p>
              Now suppose that on a later run, the same agent calls <code>send_email</code> at step four. Or it calls <code>web_search</code> eleven times instead of once. Or it pays a merchant it has never paid before. Those spans are just as well formed as the earlier ones. Nothing in them says the run was unusual, because unusual only means something relative to how this agent has behaved before. It is a property of the whole sequence, compared against a history, and a single span carries neither.
            </p>

            <h2>Why counting calls isn't enough</h2>
            <p>
              The spec's metrics come closest. Two histograms, <code>gen_ai.invoke_agent.tool_calls</code> and <code>gen_ai.invoke_agent.inference_calls</code>, record how many tool and model calls an agent makes per invocation. A rising count is a reasonable early sign that an agent is stuck in a loop.
            </p>
            <p>
              Many meaningful changes don't move the count, though. An agent that used to call <code>search</code>, <code>read</code> and <code>summarize</code>, and now calls <code>search</code>, <code>read</code> and <code>export</code>, makes the same number of calls. Its latency and token spend may not change either. A dashboard built only on counts, latency and tokens would show nothing wrong.
            </p>
            <p>
              This matters for security as much as for reliability. A successful prompt injection doesn't have to make an agent slower or more expensive. It only has to make the agent do something different, and that shows up in which tools the agent calls, not in how many.
            </p>

            <h2>What detecting drift requires</h2>
            <p>
              To catch these changes, you need three things the conventions don't try to provide.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>A baseline for each agent.</strong> What is normal for a research agent is not normal for a billing agent. The baseline has to be learned from each agent's own runs, keyed on an identity the telemetry already carries.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Detectors that give the same answer every time.</strong> Sudden shifts and gradual drift are different statistical problems. CUSUM is designed for the first. Windowed Jensen-Shannon divergence over the distribution of tool calls suits the second. Neither needs a language model to guess what the agent intended, so the same input always produces the same score, and anyone can rerun the calculation to check it.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>A contract for each task.</strong> Some changes shouldn't wait for statistics. If an agent calls a tool it has never used for this kind of task, that is worth flagging the first time it happens. A new payee is a good example: by the time a distribution has shifted, the money has moved.
            </p>
            <p>
              None of this needs new instrumentation. It needs something that reads the spans agents already emit, in order, and compares them with what came before.
            </p>

            <h2>Building on a spec that's still changing</h2>
            <p>
              Every <code>gen_ai.*</code> attribute, span and metric is still at Development stability, the earliest of OpenTelemetry's three levels. In June 2026, with <a href="https://github.com/open-telemetry/semantic-conventions/releases/tag/v1.42.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">release v1.42.0</a>, the conventions moved out of the core semantic conventions repository into a dedicated one, <code>semantic-conventions-genai</code>. That repository hasn't published a tagged release yet, and its schema URL is still listed as TODO.
            </p>
            <p>
              That is no reason to wait, but it does suggest two habits. First, keep the attribute names you depend on in one place in your code. If <code>gen_ai.tool.name</code> is renamed, you want to change one line, not search through every query and dashboard.
            </p>
            <p>
              Second, read only what you need. A behavioral score needs to know which tool was called, when, in which session and whether it succeeded. It doesn't need the prompt. If you extract those few fields and ignore everything else, you keep the spec's no-content default by construction, instead of relying on every downstream system to respect it.
            </p>

            <h2>Where this leaves you</h2>
            <p>
              The GenAI conventions are well on their way to solving the naming problem. Agents built on different frameworks can describe their work in one language, to any backend. That is a solid foundation.
            </p>
            <p>
              The next layer is judgment. We think it should be deterministic: each run measured against that agent's own history, with a number as the result, rather than a second model's opinion of the first. A trace records what happened. A score tells you whether that was normal for this agent.
            </p>

            <!-- KYDE callout -->
            <div class="not-prose mt-12 border border-line-0 bg-bg-1 p-8">
              <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-2 mb-3">KYDE Score</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-ink-1 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Kyde Score works this way. It reads <code>gen_ai.*</code> tool spans and Claude Code tool events over OTLP, learns a baseline from each agent's first 30 runs over at least 3 days, and scores every later run from 0 to 100 using CUSUM, Jensen-Shannon divergence and a per-task contract. When an agent's score moves into a different band, it sends an HMAC-signed webhook. It never reads prompt content.
              </p>
              <a
                href="/score/"
                class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-white px-5 py-2.5 text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-[0.1em] whitespace-nowrap text-cta-ink hover:bg-cta-hover transition-all"
              >
                Score an agent →
              </a>
            </div>

          </article>

          <!-- Sidebar -->
